{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CggKPM3TZq9XRSi8EChd5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fixed. `split_cents(100, 3)` now returns `[34, 33, 33]`, so the full grosz is kept.\n\nThe cause was `total // people`, which drops the remainder. The fix in `money.py` uses `divmod` and gives one extra grosz to the first `remainder` people. Every share is still within one grosz of the others.\n\nIn `test_money.py` I added two tests: one checks `split_cents(100, 3) == [34, 33, 33]`, and one checks over a range of totals and group sizes that the shares sum to the total and differ by at most one. All 5 tests pass (`python3 -m unittest test_money`).\n\nI didn't commit anything. The first-people-get-the-extra-grosz rule is my choice; if you'd rather the extra grosz go to a specific person, that's a small change."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":677,"cache_read_input_tokens":20747,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":677},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"32a6fce2-b4ff-49dd-9880-fa414f54e878","uuid":"d82ae557-e950-4600-b0de-3e869f6f96ab","timestamp":"2026-11-03T16:34:10.977Z","request_id":"req_011CggKPLkrGDTyCd34sEZrX"}
